---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CdCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/CdCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="cd-calculator">
  <CdCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the CD calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The CD calculator grows the deposit with compound interest for the term of the certificate of
      deposit. The balance at maturity is <code>A = P × (1 + r ÷ m)^(m × t)</code>, where <code>P</code> is
      the deposit, <code>r</code> is the yearly rate as a decimal, <code>m</code> is the number of times
      interest compounds in a year (365, 12, 4 or 1) and <code>t</code> is the term in years. The interest
      is <code>A − P</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The annual percentage yield is <code>APY = (1 + r ÷ m)^m − 1</code>. It is the interest one year
      earns, interest on interest included, so you can compare CDs that compound at different
      frequencies. The interest after tax is the interest times one minus your tax rate.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A deposit of 10,000 at 4.5% compounded daily for 12 months grows to 10,460.25, so it earns 460.25 of
      interest. The APY is 4.60%. With monthly compounding the same CD earns 459.40, with quarterly
      compounding 457.65 and with yearly compounding 450.00.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The rate is fixed for the term and the interest stays in the CD. Taking money out before maturity
      usually costs a penalty, which the calculator does not include. Banks may count 360 or 365 days a
      year, so their figure can differ by some cents. Tax is a flat rate on the interest; in many countries
      the interest is taxed each year as it is credited.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
